# GnuchanOS - the packages the live image carries.
#
# live-build installs this list into the chroot. It holds three kinds of
# package, and they are kept together because a live image is not a package
# set with optional parts: what is not here is not on the ISO.
#
#   1. the base of a live X session - init, a shell, the X libraries, fonts,
#      the input and video drivers under XLibre;
#   2. the tools the Gnuchan programs are BUILT with, because the hook
#      compiles them inside the image (the programs' own makefiles would ask
#      for these one at a time; naming them here is one apt run instead of
#      ten, and it is the same list either way);
#   3. small programs the session needs to be usable out of the box.
#
# XLibre itself is NOT here: it is not in Debian, so it is added by a hook
# from its own repository before this list is installed.

# --- a bootable live system ---
live-boot
live-config
live-config-systemd
systemd
systemd-sysv
dbus

# --- the boot splash. plymouth is what draws the GnuchanOS theme the Plymouth
#     hook installs; without the daemon and its script module the hook's theme
#     has nothing to draw it and the boot falls back to text. The initramfs
#     tools are what put the theme inside the initramfs, where plymouth runs
#     from before the root filesystem is mounted. ---
plymouth
plymouth-themes
initramfs-tools
# zstd is what initramfs-tools compresses the initramfs WITH when it is here,
# and without it the build says so and falls back to gzip:
#
#     W: No zstd in /usr/bin:/sbin:/bin, using gzip
#
# That warning is not fatal, which is exactly why it is worth fixing rather
# than living with: the image still boots, but the initramfs it boots from is
# the gzip one - markedly larger, and slower to decompress at boot on the
# hardware a live USB is usually plugged into. zstd is a few hundred kilobytes
# on the image and is the compression the distribution itself defaults to.
zstd

# --- GRUB. This block is the INSTALLER's boot loader, and grub-common alone was
#     never enough for it. The install runs `grub-install` to write the boot code
#     to the target disk, and `grub-install` is NOT in grub-common. Where each
#     piece lives:
#
#       grub-common        shared files and the theme tools; also what
#                          dotfile/GRUB_THEME/settings_grub.py needs to render
#                          the GnuChanOS GRUB theme (it REFUSES on a machine
#                          with no GRUB, so this stays)
#       grub2-common       /usr/sbin/grub-install, grub-mkconfig, grub-mkimage,
#                          grub-editenv - the PROGRAMS the installer runs
#       grub-pc-bin        /usr/lib/grub/i386-pc - the BIOS modules grub-install
#                          writes into the MBR
#       grub-efi-amd64-bin /usr/lib/grub/x86_64-efi - the EFI modules it writes
#                          into the ESP (efiBootLoader: grub in bootloader.conf)
#
#     Without these the installer reaches the bootloader step, cannot find
#     grub-install, and stops - after the disk is already partitioned and the
#     filesystem written, so the machine does not boot and the install cannot
#     finish. The -bin packages are named rather than the grub-pc and
#     grub-efi-amd64 META packages on purpose: the meta packages' postinst runs
#     grub-install against the BUILD machine's own disks, which is never what a
#     chroot wants, while the -bin packages only carry the modules.
#
#     (os-prober, which the module uses to find other systems for the menu, is
#     pulled in by grub2-common's own dependency.) ---
grub-common
grub2-common
grub-pc-bin
grub-efi-amd64-bin
# The disk tools the installer's partition step drives. They are the commands
# the script calls, and their absence is invisible until a step needs one - the
# install then stops with the command's own error and no way to go on.
#
#   parted, gdisk     partprobe and the GPT tools that make the kernel re-read a
#                     table it just changed
#   btrfs-progs       mkfs.btrfs - for a / on btrfs
#   xfsprogs          mkfs.xfs   - for a / on xfs
#   f2fs-tools        mkfs.f2fs  - for a / on f2fs (the script's default is
#                     ext4, which needs none of these - e2fsprogs) ---
parted
gdisk
btrfs-progs
xfsprogs
f2fs-tools
# dosfstools is mkfs.fat / mkfs.vfat, and it is what makes the EFI System
# Partition. An EFI install MUST create the ESP as FAT32; the script calls
# mkfs.fat for exactly that, and with no dosfstools the EFI path stops at "the
# partition could not be created". ext4 (the default) does not need it - this is
# the EFI path's own requirement.
dosfstools
# efibootmgr is what writes the BOOT ENTRY INTO THE FIRMWARE, and it is the piece
# whose absence is quietest and worst. On a UEFI machine grub-install COPIES the
# GRUB files onto the ESP and then calls efibootmgr to add an NVRAM entry that
# tells the firmware what to run. Without efibootmgr the copy still succeeds -
# the files are there - but nothing points the firmware at them, so the machine
# boots straight past GnuchanOS into whatever else is on the disk, or nowhere.
# The install "succeeds" and the reboot shows the old system. Named here because
# nothing else pulls it in and the build passes --apt-recommends false.
efibootmgr
# cryptsetup is the LUKS tooling. The installer's default is a plain ext4 root,
# but LUKS is a layout a script or a hand-run of the parts may use, and it is a
# small dependency to carry for the case.
cryptsetup

# --- the X server's own parts (XLibre provides the server; these are what it
#     is built against and what its drivers need) ---
xkb-data
x11-xkb-utils
x11-utils
x11-xserver-utils
xauth
xinit
keyboard-configuration
# The touchpad: the library, the X driver that loads it, and the two tools the
# touchpad configuration is applied with.
#
#   libinput10                 the libinput shared library. Everything below
#                              links against it: the X driver, a Wayland
#                              compositor, and the Gnuchan programs that read
#                              input themselves.
#   xserver-xorg-input-libinput the X server's INPUT DRIVER. This is the piece
#                              that is easy to miss and fatal to miss: the
#                              touchpad configuration at
#                              /etc/X11/xorg.conf.d/90-gnuchan-touchpad.conf
#                              names Driver "libinput", and with no driver by
#                              that name on the image the X server ignores the
#                              file and the pad falls back to its defaults -
#                              no tap to click, no two-finger scroll, no middle
#                              button. The libinput LIBRARY above is not the
#                              driver and does not stand in for it.
#   xinput                     the tool that pushes a touchpad setting into the
#                              session that is running. The X server reads its
#                              configuration once, at startup, so without this
#                              a setting written now takes effect only at the
#                              next login. Both the window manager (wm_input.c)
#                              and dotfile/TOUCHPAD_SETTINGS apply settings
#                              through it.
#   libinput-tools             the `libinput` command, for reading a device's
#                              capabilities when a pad does not behave.
libinput10
xserver-xorg-input-libinput
xinput
libinput-tools
udev
libgl1
libgl1-mesa-dri
libegl1
libgbm1
procps
pciutils
usbutils

# --- fonts ---
xfonts-base
fonts-dejavu-core
fonts-dejavu-extra
fonts-liberation2

# --- the tools the Gnuchan programs are built with ---
build-essential
pkg-config
python3
python3-setuptools
ca-certificates
curl
gnupg
git
# rsync is here because a step INSIDE the chroot calls it, and a chroot that
# does not have it answers exactly
#
#     rsync failed with error code 127
#
# which is the shell's "command not found" - rsync is simply not installed. The
# build's own host-side copy (build.sh installs rsync on the build machine) is
# NOT this: it is the copy live-build uses to move the overlay into the chroot,
# and it lives on the HOST. A program's build helper or a hook that runs in the
# chroot looks for rsync in the chroot and there was none, so every such call
# failed with 127. Naming it here is what puts it on the image, inside the
# chroot, where the calls actually happen.
rsync

# --- what the programs link against ---
libx11-dev
libxft-dev
libfreetype-dev
libxcursor-dev
libimlib2-dev
libxcomposite-dev
libxdamage-dev
libxrender-dev
libxrandr-dev
libxss-dev
libxext-dev
libxmu-dev
libxpm-dev
libpng-dev
libjpeg-dev
# GnuChanSS's optional D-Bus client. Without this header the screen saver
# builds WITHOUT the inhibit name, so a video (Firefox, Chromium, mpv, VLC)
# cannot tell it to hold off and the saver drops over a film once the idle
# clock passes — the exact fault the name exists to prevent. It is a build
# dependency of the live image and not only of a development machine.
libdbus-1-dev
# GnuChanDM (the display manager) authenticates through PAM, so it is built
# against libpam's header. It is listed here rather than left to the DM's own
# makefile, which would apt-install it mid-build: the image should carry what
# its programs are built from, and one apt run at image time is one fewer thing
# to go wrong inside the chroot.
libpam0g-dev
libpam-modules
libpam-runtime
# The X server wrapper that reads Xwrapper.config. It is what lets a normal
# user start X, which is how the live session comes up (see the session hook).
xserver-xorg-legacy
# dbus-x11 provides the session bus helpers a desktop session expects; the
# notification server and every program that talks to it want a session bus.
dbus-x11
# The user-session machinery. Both of these are Recommends - libpam-systemd of
# systemd, dbus-user-session of dbus - so --apt-recommends false drops them, and
# without them the graphical session has no runtime directory and no session
# bus. The chain: pam_systemd, which lives in libpam-systemd, registers the login
# with systemd-logind, and logind is what creates /run/user/$UID and starts the
# user's D-Bus. dbus-user-session is what puts the bus socket at
# $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/bus that the environment points at. GnuChanDM starts the
# session itself and looks for exactly those two paths (see dm_session.c): with
# the packages absent they never exist, so the notification server holds its
# name on no bus and every program that wants one either gives up or spawns an
# invisible bus of its own.
libpam-systemd
dbus-user-session

# --- GnuChanBrowser is a Qt program ---
qt6-base-dev
qt6-base-dev-tools
libqt6widgets6
libqt6network6
libqt6webenginewidgets6
libqt6webenginecore6

# --- networking, so the live image is actually usable online ---
# NetworkManager is what gives `nmcli`, the little applet in the bar and the
# whole connect-to-wifi flow. It is not optional on a live image: a machine
# that boots with no way to join a network is a machine that can do nothing
# with the network it was downloaded onto. wpasupplicant is what NetworkManager
# drives to authenticate a WPA network; without it every secured network is
# refused with no visible reason.
network-manager
wpasupplicant
wireless-tools
rfkill
# ping and `ip`, named explicitly. Both are Priority: important and come in
# with the standard base, so with the default debootstrap variant they are on
# the image already - but naming them is deliberate. An earlier build passed
# --variant=minbase, which installs only ESSENTIAL packages and silently
# dropped the whole important layer: the image booted with no `ip` and no
# `ping`, and a wireless link that associated and took a lease passed nothing,
# because the tools that put an address and a route on the interface were not
# there. The variant is fixed in build.sh; these two lines make the tools the
# Gnuchan programs actually call (net_device.c runs `ip -o -4 addr show`,
# net_ui.c runs `ip link set`) survive any future change to that variant.
iputils-ping
iproute2
# systemd-resolved is the resolver GnuChanNetworkManager drives with resolvectl,
# and the image was going without it: `resolvectl` answered "command not found",
# so the manager's per-link DNS path had nothing to call and every lookup left
# the machine as plain port-53 traffic. That matters beyond tidiness — on a
# network that filters DNS BY NAME (a blocked site answers google.com but
# refuses discord.com from the same 8.8.8.8), plain DNS cannot be escaped by
# changing servers, and the only way out is encrypted DNS. systemd-resolved is
# what provides it: DNS-over-TLS, set with `resolvectl dnsovertls <link> yes`,
# which encrypts the query so a middlebox cannot read the name and refuse it.
# It is also what makes the manager's own resolvectl commands work at all.
systemd-resolved
# The kernel firmware the wireless cards need. The firmware is not optional and
# is easy to leave out by accident: a card with no firmware has NO interface at
# all, so `nmcli` shows nothing to connect to and the card looks like it is not
# supported. firmware-linux pulls the lot; the named ones are the common
# wireless chipsets and are listed so a build that trims the metapackage still
# carries them.
firmware-linux
firmware-iwlwifi
firmware-atheros
firmware-realtek
firmware-brcm80211
firmware-ralink
firmware-misc-nonfree

# The kernel firmware the GRAPHICS cards need. This block exists for the same
# reason as the wireless one above and was the same omission: a GPU whose
# firmware is not on the image does not come up, and the machine falls back to
# software rendering (llvmpipe) or a black screen - which looks like "this
# distribution does not support my card". Each of the three vendors is named
# separately even though firmware-linux pulls most of them, so that a trimmed
# metapackage still carries them.
#
#   firmware-amd-graphics    every modern AMD card and Ryzen APU needs this to
#                            light up at all; without it amdgpu cannot bring
#                            the card out of reset.
#   firmware-nvidia-graphics  what nouveau loads on Pascal (GTX 10xx) and
#                            newer; without it those cards stay on the VESA
#                            framebuffer.
#   firmware-intel-graphics  GuC/HuC blobs for Skylake and newer Intel
#                            graphics; older Intel (965GM and the like) needs
#                            none.
firmware-amd-graphics
firmware-nvidia-graphics
firmware-intel-graphics
# The GPU-side userspace. libgl1-mesa-dri (already listed) is the OpenGL
# driver for every vendor; these three are the Vulkan loader and the per-vendor
# Vulkan drivers, so an application that asks for Vulkan (a game, an emulator)
# finds it instead of falling back to OpenGL or failing.
libvulkan1
mesa-vulkan-drivers

# --- the torrent client and its bindings, from dotfile/Install_Software.py.
#     deluge is the client, and its daemon (deluged) and web UI ship inside it;
#     deluge-gtk is the windowed front end; python3-libtorrent is the library
#     the client runs on. They are named here so the live image carries what a
#     machine that ran Install_Software.py carries, and the services that the
#     script turns on (ssh, irqbalance, ntpsec) are enabled by the
#     0250-install-software hook. ---
deluge
deluge-gtk
python3-libtorrent

# --- the session's helpers: sound, a browser backend, the pointer theme ---
alsa-utils
xterm
x11-apps
nano
sudo
irqbalance
ntpsec
openssh-server

# --- locale and time ---
locales
tzdata

# --- the installer ----------------------------------------------------------
#
# THE INSTALLER IS THIS REPOSITORY'S OWN PROGRAM, NOT A DEBIAN PACKAGE, so there
# is no `calamares` line here any more. Calamares used to be the installer and
# was dropped: its bootloader module read a config key its file did not set and
# died, it reported a failed grub-install as "returned error code 1" with the
# real reason sent to a log file and not the screen, and its display manager
# list does not know GnuChanDM.
#
# The replacement is a program of this repository, installer/, in two halves:
#
#   installer/src/          GnuChanInstall, the WINDOW - an X11 program built
#                           with Xlib and Xft exactly like GnuChanSL, GnuChanDM
#                           and GnuChanDock. It asks which disk and the account's
#                           name and password and shows the install's output.
#                           Its makefile.py compiles it in the chroot and
#                           installs it; the 0450-installer hook runs it.
#   installer/gnuchanos-install.sh
#                           the INSTALL itself: one shell script the window runs
#                           with the answers, that partitions, copies the
#                           filesystem, writes the boot loader and makes the
#                           account, printing every step.
#
# The window is built with the SAME X11 and Xft libraries the other Gnuchan
# programs are, so libx11-dev/libxft-dev above (and the fonts and libx11-6 the
# X session pulls in) are all it needs - nothing new is required for it.
#
# WHAT THE SCRIPT NEEDS, and where each comes from (all of these are also
# pulled in by the base system, and are named here so the installer does not
# depend on that staying true):
#
#   squashfs-tools  `unsquashfs`, which writes the live system onto the disk -
#                   /run/live/medium/live/filesystem.squashfs is a squashfs and
#                   this is the only thing that can read it. THE WHOLE INSTALL
#                   DEPENDS ON IT; without it the script stops before touching
#                   the disk.
#   util-linux      `sfdisk` (partitioning), `wipefs`, `lsblk`, `blkid`,
#                   `mount`/`umount`, `findmnt`
#   e2fsprogs       `mkfs.ext4`
#   parted          `partprobe`, and the partition-table tools the script calls
#   dosfstools      `mkfs.fat`, for the EFI system partition on a UEFI machine
#   efibootmgr      what grub-install calls to write the firmware boot entry
#   initramfs-tools `update-initramfs`, to rebuild the initramfs for the target
squashfs-tools
util-linux
e2fsprogs
parted
dosfstools
efibootmgr
initramfs-tools
